The choices can also be written as a dynamic programming table. `dp[left][right]` stores the best score after taking `left` cards from the beginning and `right` cards from the end. From each state, the next card can come from either side.

This is not the fastest version, but the table makes the two possible moves clear. The code also works when `k` is zero because the starting state already contains the answer.

```java
class Solution {
    public int maxScore(int[] cardScore, int k) {
        int n = cardScore.length;
        int[][] dp = new int[k + 1][k + 1];
        boolean[][] seen = new boolean[k + 1][k + 1];

        dp[0][0] = 0;
        seen[0][0] = true;

        for (int left = 0; left <= k; left++) {
            for (int right = 0; right <= k - left; right++) {
                if (!seen[left][right]) {
                    continue;
                }

                int taken = left + right;
                if (taken == k) {
                    continue;
                }

                if (left < k) {
                    dp[left + 1][right] = Math.max(
                        dp[left + 1][right],
                        dp[left][right] + cardScore[left]
                    );
                    seen[left + 1][right] = true;
                }

                if (right < k) {
                    int rightIndex = n - 1 - right;
                    dp[left][right + 1] = Math.max(
                        dp[left][right + 1],
                        dp[left][right] + cardScore[rightIndex]
                    );
                    seen[left][right + 1] = true;
                }
            }
        }

        int answer = 0;
        for (int left = 0; left <= k; left++) {
            int right = k - left;
            answer = Math.max(answer, dp[left][right]);
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

The table uses `O(k^2)` time and `O(k^2)` space. Unlike the constant-space versions, it stores many intermediate choices. The array is only read, not modified.

For each possible number of cards taken from the left, the rest must come from the right. There are only `k + 1` such choices, so I calculate the two sums directly and keep the largest one.

For example, when `k = 3`, the code checks 0 left and 3 right, 1 left and 2 right, and so on. It does not change the input array.

```cpp
class Solution{
  public:
    int maxScore(vector<int>& cardScore , int k){
        int n = cardScore.size();
        long long best = 0;
        long long leftSum = 0;
        long long rightSum = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i <= k; i++) {
            if (i > 0) {
                leftSum += cardScore[i - 1];
            }

            int rightCount = k - i;
            rightSum = 0;
            for (int j = 0; j < rightCount; j++) {
                rightSum += cardScore[n - 1 - j];
            }

            best = max(best, leftSum + rightSum);
        }

        return static_cast<int>(best);
    }
};
```

The time cost is `O(k^2)` because the right side is summed again for every split. The extra space is `O(1)`. This is simple to check, but slower than the linear sliding window versions.

Start with all `k` cards on the left. Then remove the last selected left card and add one card from the right. Repeating this gives every valid split without recalculating the whole sum.

The selected cards never overlap because the removed left index moves toward zero while the right index moves backward from the end. If `k` equals the array length, the loop simply ends with the sum of every card.

```cpp
class Solution{
  public:
    int maxScore(vector<int>& cardScore , int k){
        int n = cardScore.size();
        long long current = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
            current += cardScore[i];
        }

        long long answer = current;
        int leftIndex = k - 1;
        int rightIndex = n - 1;

        while (leftIndex >= 0) {
            current -= cardScore[leftIndex];
            current += cardScore[rightIndex];
            answer = max(answer, current);
            leftIndex--;
            rightIndex--;
        }

        return static_cast<int>(answer);
    }
};
```

For `[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 1]` and `k = 3`, the running scores are 6, 4, 8, and 12. The last value is the best one.

The time complexity is `O(k)` and the extra space is `O(1)`. No values are changed in `cardScore`.

The usable cards are the last `k` cards followed by the first `k` cards. Imagine joining those two parts into a circle. Every group of `k` consecutive values in this short circular view represents one legal choice.

The code uses `% n` instead of building another array. It checks `2k` positions, so it also handles taking all cards when `k` is equal to `n`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int maxScore(int[] cardScore, int k) {
        int n = cardScore.length;
        if (k == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        long windowSum = 0;
        long answer = 0;
        int start = n - k;

        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
            windowSum += cardScore[(start + i) % n];
        }
        answer = windowSum;

        for (int i = 1; i < k; i++) {
            int outgoing = (start + i - 1) % n;
            int incoming = (start + i + k - 1) % n;
            windowSum -= cardScore[outgoing];
            windowSum += cardScore[incoming];
            answer = Math.max(answer, windowSum);
        }

        return (int) answer;
    }
}
```

The time complexity is `O(k)` and the extra space is `O(1)`. A single card is never counted twice inside any checked window, and the input array is not changed.

## Intuition

The idea was pretty straightforward.
First, take the sum of the first `k` cards because initially we are choosing all cards from the left side.

Now instead of checking every possible combination separately, we slowly start shifting our choice:

* remove one card from the left side,
* add one card from the right side.

This way, we generate all possible ways of picking `k` cards from both ends.

For every shift, we simply keep track of the maximum sum obtained.

---

## Approach

1. Calculate the sum of the first `k` cards.
2. Store this as the initial answer.
3. Start iterating from `1` to `k`:

   * remove the `(k - i)`th card from the left-picked portion,
   * add the `(n - i)`th card from the right side.
4. Update the maximum answer after every shift.
5. Return the maximum sum.

The important thing here is:

* `currSum` should always represent the current configuration sum,
* while `ans` stores the best sum found so far.

---

## Solution

```java
class Solution {
  public int maxScore(int[] cardScore, int k) {
    int currSum = 0;
    int n = cardScore.length;

    // Initial sum taking first k cards
    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
      currSum += cardScore[i];
    }

    int ans = currSum;

    // Gradually shift picks from left to right
    for (int i = 1; i <= k; i++) {
      currSum = currSum - cardScore[k - i] + cardScore[n - i];
      ans = Math.max(ans, currSum);
    }

    return ans;
  }
}
```

---

## Complexity

**Time Complexity:**
`O(k)` because we iterate through at most `k` elements twice.

**Space Complexity:**
`O(1)` since we are only using a few extra variables and no additional data structures.

